  "completion": "<think>\nOkay, so I need to forecast the probability that by each given deadline, a Millennium Prize Problem (excluding Poincar\u00e9 which was already solved) is fully solved with a key contribution from AI. The deadlines are in 2026, 2030, 2035, 2040, and 2050. The probabilities have to be non-decreasing and between 0 and 1.\n\nFirst, I should consider the nature of the problems. These are all deeply complex, longstanding problems in mathematics. For example, P vs NP is notoriously difficult; it's been open for over 50 years. The Riemann Hypothesis has also resisted proof for over a century. Solving any of these would likely require breakthroughs in mathematical theory or techniques.\n\nAI's current capabilities in mathematics are still somewhat limited. While AI can assist in generating hypotheses and exploring mathematical structures (like the work on the Riemann Hypothesis using ML), it's not yet clear how AI would contribute a crucial new proof step in these problems. AI's role might involve processing vast data, discovering patterns, or even generating part of a proof sketch for humans to refine. However, the final step of a proof for such problems is likely to require deep human insight and creativity, which AI may not fully replicate.\n\nBy 2026 (just 1 year after the forecast date), the probability is probably very low. A year seems unlikely for such a significant breakthrough, even with AI. Maybe 1-2% chance.\n\nBy 2030 (5 years later), more time allows for AI to be integrated more into mathematical research. If certain problems become more accessible through AI's assistance, maybe 10% chance. But I'm not sure if AI will have made substantial contributions by then.\n\n2035 (10 years later) \u2013 if AI systems progress to a level where they can assist in proof construction, perhaps via collaborative efforts between humans and AI. That might push the probability up to 20-30%. However, 30% perhaps enough as a midpoint.\n\nBy 2040 (15 years)