is relatively rare (since these are hard problems), but given AI's potential future capabilities, the probability increases over time but insignificantly. For example, even by 2050, maybe a 30% chance. But I suspect the non-AI-assisted progress is minimal. Adding AI could add a small amount. However, maybe AI does contribute significantly in the next 25 years. But I'm not sure.\n\nGiven that, perhaps the probabilities need to be flat when considering that the problem might not be solved by humans, and AI might not contribute anything. But the question is when AI contributes to at least one solution.\n\nSo for 2026, next year: perhaps 1% (2026 is 2025+1) \u2013 very low. For 2030 (5 years), 5%. 2035 (10 years) 15%, 2040 (15 years) 30%, 2050 (25 years) 50%. That seems like an increasing trend. The data might be limited, but I think AI's role will grow, thus increasing the chances over time.\n\nSo the JSON would be:\n\n{\n  \"2026\": 0.01,\n  \"2030\": 0.05,\n  \"2035\": 0.1,\n  \"2040\": 0.25,\n  \"2050\": 0.4\n}\n\nWait, but the numbers might not be smooth. Let me check the steps. 1%, 5%, 10%, 25%, 50% would be an increase, but the user didn't require steps to be smooth just non-decreasing. But perhaps I should adjust to make the increases more reasonable.\n\nAlternatively, maybe 0.01, 0.02, 0.05, 0.15, 0.3. But that's more gradual.\n\nAlternatively, considering the payout time, 2026 is 2025 + 1 year. I think the probabilities should increase as the deadlines recede. So for 2026, 0.01; 2030: 0.02; 2035: 0.05; 2040: 0.1; 2050: 0.2. But that might be too gradual.\n\nAnother way to think: the chance of success is low, but increases over time. Maybe by 2040, 20% chance. By 2050, 30% chance. However, that might be too low.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps 2026: 0.01, 2030: 0.01, 2035: 0.01, 2040: 0.05, 2050: 0.1. But that might be considered as non-increasing, which is not allowed. Need to make sure each is non-decreasing. So, all probabilities must be at least as large as the p